<div class="article-content prose" style="margin-top: 2.5rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid var(--line);">
  <h2>Step-by-Step Calculation Formula &amp; Worked Example</h2>
  <p>Amex requires that transfers occur in blocks of 1,000 points. Any points remaining below the unit block remain in your Membership Rewards balance:</p>
  
  <div class="formula-box" style="margin: 1.5rem 0; padding: 1.25rem; background: var(--ink-2); border-left: 4px solid var(--gold); border-radius: 6px;">
    <div><strong>Transferable Points</strong> = Floor(Total Points ÷ 1,000) × 1,000 (minimum 1,000)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Aeroplan Points Received</strong> = Transferable Points × 1.0 × (1 + Promotional Bonus %)</div>
    <div><strong>Effective Miles per Point</strong> = 1.0 × (1 + Promotional Bonus %)</div>
    <div style="margin-top: 0.5rem;"><strong>Points Remaining in Amex</strong> = Total Points - Transferable Points</div>
  </div>

  <h3>Worked Real-World Example</h3>
  <p>Suppose you have <strong>60,800 Amex Membership Rewards points</strong> and an active <strong>15% hypothetical transfer bonus promotion</strong>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Eligible Transferable Block</strong>: 60,000 points (800 points stay in your Amex balance).</li>
    <li><strong>Base Points (1:1 base ratio)</strong>: 60,000 Aeroplan points.</li>
    <li><strong>Promotional Bonus (15% assumption)</strong>: 9,000 bonus Aeroplan points.</li>
    <li><strong>Total Received</strong>: <strong>69,000 Aeroplan points</strong> (effective multiplier: <strong>1.15</strong>).</li>
    <li><strong>Estimated Travel Purchasing Power</strong>: At an illustrative 1.5¢ benchmark valuation assumption, 69,000 Aeroplan points yield approximately <strong>$1,035</strong> in travel purchasing power. (This valuation is an illustrative modeling assumption, not a guaranteed cash price).</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Transfer Rules, Fees, and Friction Points</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Excise Fee Offset</strong>: <strong>$0 Free</strong>. Under US tax regulations, Amex charges an Excise Tax Offset Fee of $0.0006 per point (capped at $99) only when transferring to US domestic airlines (like Delta). Because Air Canada is based in Canada, <strong>no fee is charged</strong>.</li>
    <li><strong>Transfer Speed</strong>: Transfers typically process <strong>instantly</strong> under standard conditions. In rare instances involving newly linked accounts, fraud screening can take up to 24–48 hours.</li>
    <li><strong>No Fuel Surcharges</strong>: A major advantage of Air Canada Aeroplan is that it does not pass on carrier-imposed surcharges (YQ/YR) on partner redemptions (such as Lufthansa, ANA, Swiss, or Singapore Airlines), keeping your cash out-of-pocket costs remarkably low.</li>
    <li><strong>One-Way Irreversibility</strong>: Once transferred, Aeroplan points cannot normally be returned to Amex. Always confirm seat availability before initiating the transfer.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Valuation Trade-Off: Is Transferring Amex to Aeroplan Worth It?</h2>
  <p>Both Amex Membership Rewards points and Air Canada Aeroplan points are commonly modeled with an editorial reference around <strong>1.5¢ per point</strong>. However, identical baseline assumptions do not by themselves prove that a given transfer is advantageous.</p>
  <p>Transferring to Aeroplan creates genuine outsized value when:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Transatlantic &amp; Transpacific Business Class</strong>: Booking Star Alliance partner premium cabins where cash airfares exceed $3,500+ and fuel surcharges are waived, delivering CPP well above 2.0¢–3.5¢.</li>
    <li><strong>Stopover Bookings</strong>: Adding an international stopover on a one-way award ticket for just 5,000 additional Aeroplan points.</li>
    <li><strong>Promotional Bonuses</strong>: Taking advantage of a 15%–20% bonus to amplify purchasing power, lowering the effective points required for your ticket.</li>
  </ol>

